1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Ne4 $5 {Much better than its reputation as I was about to discover!} 5. Nxe4 dxe4 6. Be3 {To stop ...c5, but Black plays it anyway!} c5 $1 7. dxc5 Nd7 8. f4 ({Tim Harding in 'The Classical French' (Batsford 1991) says to "avoid} 8. b4 $2 b6 9. Qd4 bxc5 10. Qxe4 Rb8 11. b5 Bb7 {, with a clear advantage, Filipowicz-Pytel, Polish Championship 1973."}) ({The main move is} 8. Qg4) 8... exf3 9. Nxf3 Qc7 ({Harding gives only} 9... Bxc5 {and quotes Przewoznik and Konca as awarding Black a slight edge, but I can't see what's wrong with White after} 10. Bxc5 Nxc5 (10... Qa5+ $4 11. b4) 11. Qxd8+ Kxd8 12. O-O-O+) 10. Bd3 $5 Bxc5 11. Qe2 ({I'd decided on this pawn sac on the previous move. However,} 11. Bf4 Qb6 12. Rb1 Bf2+ 13. Ke2 {may not be as silly for White as it looks at first glance.}) 11... Nxe5 12. O-O-O Nxd3+ 13. Rxd3 Bxe3+ 14. Qxe3 Bd7 15. Rhd1 ({Too mechanical. Better is} 15. Qd4 $1 {e.g.} f6 ({Black probably does best to give the pawn back by} 15... Rd8 16. Qxa7 O-O {, when his bishop and passed pawn give him an edge}) 16. Rhd1 Bc6 17. Re3 Kf7 (17... e5 $2 18. Nxe5 $1) 18. Rde1) 15... Bc6 16. Ne5 O-O 17. Qd4 {Taking control of the open file and eyeing g7.} Rac8 18. Nxc6 ({Not} 18. Rg3 Bf3 $1 ({Stockfish 17:} 18... Bd5 $1 $19) 19. Rd2 Rfd8 {(Stockfish 17:} 20. Nd7 $1 $11 {)}) 18... Qxc6 19. Rc3 ({Correct is} 19. R1d2 {as the text allows 19...Qxg2!}) {Instead of 19...Qxg2! Black played the horrendous} 19... Qa6 $4 (19... Qxg2 $1 20. Rxc8 (20. Rg1 {is met by} e5) {is well met by} 20... Qg5+ $1 {covering the mate at d8 after} 21. Kb1 Rxc8) 20. Rxc8 {Source: bulletin; Chess Circuit No.6, 1994, p9. White's national grade was 161 (equivalent to Elo 1888)} 1-0

